Hannah Brewer’s first sale was a pair of leather boots she had worn twice. She listed them on a resale app for £40, mostly to clear some space in her wardrobe, and they were gone in eleven minutes.

{{41}}

That gap between what clothes cost new and what they fetch second-hand is the foundation of an industry now worth billions. Fast fashion has made garments so cheap that many are worn only a handful of times, which has left millions of nearly new items sitting in wardrobes across the country.

{{42}}

Hannah’s solution was to take that effort away from the seller. She began collecting bags of clothes from friends, and then from friends of friends, promising to photograph, describe and post everything herself in return for a share of the sale price.

{{43}}

Plain, well-made pieces in neutral colours consistently outperformed trendy ones, which tend to lose their appeal within a season. Condition mattered more than the label, and a coat with a missing button could sit unsold for months while an identical one sold overnight.

{{44}}

The solution turned out to be a partnership with a local charity shop, which agreed to pass on its best donations in exchange for a fixed monthly fee. Hannah gained a reliable supply at almost no cost, and the charity earned money from items it had no space to display.

{{45}}

Hannah accepts the criticism up to a point, but argues that a jacket resold six times is worth more to everyone than one thrown away after a single winter. The real question, she says, is not who profits from each sale but how many owners each garment manages to have.

